d) VMware Networking

Practiced different VMware network modes.

i) NAT

NAT allows the virtual machine to access the internet through the host computer's network connection.

Ubuntu VM
    |
    | NAT
    ↓
Windows Host
    |
    ↓
Internet

Useful for normal internet access from the VM.

ii) Bridged

In Bridged mode, the VM connects to the physical network as a separate device.

Ubuntu VM
    |
    ↓
Physical Network
    |
    ↓
Router / Network

The VM can receive its own IP address from the network.

iii) Host-only

Host-only creates a private network between the Windows host and the VM.

Windows Host
     |
     | Host-only Network
     |
 Ubuntu VM

Useful for isolated testing and lab environments.

e) VMware Snapshots

Practiced creating and restoring VMware snapshots before making configuration changes.

Example workflow:

Ubuntu VM
    ↓
Create Snapshot
    ↓
Make Configuration Changes
    ↓
Test Changes
    ↓
Problem?
    ↓
Revert to Snapshot

Snapshots were used as a convenient recovery point during lab testing.

Learning

A snapshot captures the VM's state at a particular point in time and can be used to revert the VM during lab testing.

Note: Snapshots are not a replacement for proper backups.

f) VM Cloning

Practiced cloning an Ubuntu virtual machine to create a second VM for testing.

Ubuntu VM
    |
    | Clone
    ↓
Ubuntu VM 2

After cloning, learned the importance of configuring a unique hostname for the cloned VM.

Example:

hostname

Change hostname using:

sudo hostnamectl set-hostname ubuntu-vm2

Verify:

hostname

